{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The select is the native <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> wearing the
     field-family clothes: same 44px box, same label/helper/error markup,
     with a chevron drawn in CSS instead of the platform arrow. It exists
     because a visible radio group stops scaling past a handful of options —
     the select is the honest fallback, not a styled imitation.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>One choice from <strong>more than ~5 options</strong> — regions,
        countries, sort orders, plan tiers.</li>
    <li>The option list is <strong>long or data-driven</strong>; a native
        select scrolls and type-aheads for free.</li>
    <li>It sits in a form next to text fields — the shared
        <code>fl-field</code> markup keeps the rhythm identical.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>2–5 options — that is a
        <a href="selection-controls.html">radio group</a>; visible options
        beat a hidden dropdown.</li>
    <li>You need a <strong>custom menu</strong> (icons, search, multi-line
        options) — that is the listbox pattern, planned for 0.4/0.5 with the
        overlay infrastructure. Don't hand-roll it; that's how libraries
        grow 300 lines of fragile focus-management JS.</li>
    <li>Multiple selection — <code>multiple</code> selects are not styled;
        use a checkbox group.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "The popup is browser-controlled — on purpose",
    text: "appearance:none only reshapes the closed control. The open dropdown (its panel, rows and highlight) is drawn by the browser/OS. That keeps type-ahead, scroll, long lists and mobile pickers correct with zero JS. Flint will not restyle the popup until the custom listbox lands with proper focus management.",
    href: "selection-controls.html",
    linkText: "Selection controls"
  }) }}
</section>

